<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/>
<style>
  body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;margin:0;padding:20px;color:#111;}
  .cover{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#4f46e5,#7c3aed);color:#fff;padding:30px 36px;border-radius:12px;margin-bottom:24px;}
  .cover h1{font-size:26px;font-weight:800;margin:0 0 6px;}
  .cover .sub{opacity:.8;font-size:14px;}
  .meta-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:12px;background:#f9fafb;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;border-radius:10px;padding:16px;margin-bottom:22px;}
  .meta-item label{display:block;font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#9ca3af;letter-spacing:.8px;margin-bottom:4px;}
  .meta-item span{font-size:16px;font-weight:800;color:#111827;}
  table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;}
  th{background:#4f46e5;color:#fff;padding:10px 12px;text-align:left;font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.5px;border:1px solid #4338ca;}
  @media print{body{padding:10px;} .cover{-webkit-print-color-adjust:exact;print-color-adjust:exact;} th{-webkit-print-color-adjust:exact;print-color-adjust:exact;}}
</style></head><body>
<div class="cover">
  <h1>📋 Vcube Exam — Answer Sheet</h1>
  <div class="sub">Vcube Software Solutions · Multi Cloud DevSecOps Program</div>
</div>
<div class="meta-grid">
  <div class="meta-item"><label>Student</label><span>Mohammed Abdul Sami</span></div>
  <div class="meta-item"><label>Batch</label><span>AWS Batch 18</span></div>
  <div class="meta-item"><label>Score</label><span>23/25</span></div>
  <div class="meta-item"><label>Result</label><span>92% · 🏆 Outstanding</span></div>
</div>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th style="width:40px">#</th><th>Question · Your Answer · Correct Answer · Explanation</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">1</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which of the following IP addresses belongs to a private (RFC 1918) range?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">172.20.5.10</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">172.20.5.10</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> 172.16.0.0/12 (172.16-172.31) is private. 8.8.8.8 and 1.1.1.1 are public DNS; 100.64.0.0/10 is carrier-grade NAT space, not RFC 1918.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">2</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which target types can you register behind a Network Load Balancer target group?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">EC2 instances, IP addresses, and even an Application Load Balancer</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">EC2 instances, IP addresses, and even an Application Load Balancer</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> NLB supports instance and IP targets, and you can front an ALB with an NLB to combine static IPs with Layer 7 features.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">3</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Approximately how many total IP addresses does a 10.0.0.0/16 network contain?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">65,536</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">65,536</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> A /16 leaves 16 bits for hosts: 2^16 = 65,536 addresses (the /16 VPC CIDR you see in AWS examples).</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">4</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">In a hub-and-spoke AWS network design, which service acts as the central hub connecting many VPCs and on-premises networks?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">AWS Transit Gateway</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">AWS Transit Gateway</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Transit Gateway is the cloud router: each VPC and VPN attaches to the hub instead of building many peer-to-peer links.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">5</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Before a VPC peering connection becomes active, what must happen?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The owner of the accepter VPC must accept the peering request</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The owner of the accepter VPC must accept the peering request</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Peering is a requester/accepter handshake; after acceptance you still must update route tables in both VPCs.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">6</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">By default, a newly created custom Security Group allows all outbound traffic.</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> New custom SGs start with no inbound rules and an outbound rule allowing 0.0.0.0/0; you then add inbound allows as needed.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">7</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">For which workload is AWS Global Accelerator often a better fit than CloudFront?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Non-HTTP TCP/UDP applications such as gaming or IoT that need static entry IPs</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Non-HTTP TCP/UDP applications such as gaming or IoT that need static entry IPs</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Global Accelerator works at the network layer for any TCP/UDP traffic and gives static Anycast IPs; CloudFront is an HTTP/S caching CDN.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">8</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">AWS Config rules can automatically flag a resource that is non-compliant, for example an unencrypted S3 bucket.</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Rules mark resources COMPLIANT or NON_COMPLIANT and can trigger automatic remediation actions.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">9</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which ALB listener rule condition sends api.example.com traffic to one target group and www.example.com to another?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Host-header (host-based) routing</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Host-header (host-based) routing</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Host-header rules look at the requested domain; path-pattern rules look at the URL path (e.g. /api/*).</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#fff5f5;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">10</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Why is CloudTrail valuable for security and auditing of an AWS account?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#dc2626">The CloudTrail is valuable for security and auditing of an AWS account, it stores all the metrics logs actions performed by an individual user no matter in the creating the VPC, IAM policies, IAM roles, and EC2 network configuration and etc. This helps security teams in identifying the criminal or any security incident is occurred like data breaches and etc.</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">CloudTrail records every API call with the caller's identity, time and source, creating an audit trail. Security teams use it to investigate incidents and unauthorized changes, and auditors use it as evidence for compliance.</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Without CloudTrail you cannot answer 'who deleted/changed this resource and when'.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#dc2626">❌ Wrong</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">11</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What is an IAM policy?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">A JSON document that defines permissions - which actions are allowed or denied on which resources</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">A JSON document that defines permissions - which actions are allowed or denied on which resources</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Policies are attached to users, groups and roles; they state Effect (Allow/Deny), Action and Resource.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">12</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What does an EC2 Auto Scaling Group do?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Automatically adds or removes EC2 instances to maintain desired capacity and replace unhealthy instances</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Automatically adds or removes EC2 instances to maintain desired capacity and replace unhealthy instances</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> ASGs keep instance count between min/max, follow scaling policies, and swap out instances that fail health checks.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">13</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What does AWS Config do?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Continuously records the configuration of your AWS resources and evaluates them against desired rules for compliance</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Continuously records the configuration of your AWS resources and evaluates them against desired rules for compliance</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Config keeps a configuration history per resource and runs managed/custom rules (e.g. 'is encryption enabled?') against them.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">14</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What does an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection do?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Creates an encrypted IPsec tunnel over the internet between your on-premises network and your VPC</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Creates an encrypted IPsec tunnel over the internet between your on-premises network and your VPC</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Site-to-Site VPN links your data center (customer gateway) to AWS (virtual private gateway or Transit Gateway) with IPsec encryption.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">15</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">In an Auto Scaling Group, what does a launch template specify?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The configuration for new instances: AMI, instance type, key pair, security groups and user data</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The configuration for new instances: AMI, instance type, key pair, security groups and user data</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Launch templates (or the older launch configurations) tell the ASG exactly how to build each new instance.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">16</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What is CloudWatch Logs used for?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Centralizing and storing log data from EC2, Lambda and other AWS services for search and analysis</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Centralizing and storing log data from EC2, Lambda and other AWS services for search and analysis</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Logs from many services stream into log groups; you can filter, query with Logs Insights, and set metric filters on patterns.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">17</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">An Auto Scaling Group can launch instances across multiple Availability Zones for better availability.</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> You attach the ASG to subnets in several AZs and it balances instances across them.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">18</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What does AWS CloudTrail primarily record?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">API calls and account activity - who did what, when, and from where</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">API calls and account activity - who did what, when, and from where</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> CloudTrail logs management (and optionally data) events with identity, timestamp and source IP - the audit history of the account.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">19</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which S3 feature automatically moves objects to cheaper storage classes (for example to Glacier) based on rules you define?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">S3 Lifecycle policies</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">S3 Lifecycle policies</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Lifecycle rules transition objects (Standard -> IA -> Glacier) or expire them after the age you specify.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#fff5f5;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">20</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">What is a Multi-AZ deployment in Amazon RDS and why would you enable it?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#dc2626">A Multi-AZ deployment in Amazon RDS is the secondary Database server in the other or multi Availability Zones, which acts as a backup when we the primary database is down or failover due to some availability issue. In the Multi-AZ deployment, the Database servers are created in the different Availibility Zone in case of Data center connectivity issue due to wars etc. As much as I remember while doing the hands-on. There are two type of Multi-AZ deployment in Amazon RDS, 

1. 3 level Multi-AZ deployment  - it creates two secondary databases (backups) under Multi-AZ deployment in 2 different availability zones and if primary database is down, it stores in the two secondary databases as per their activeness

2. 2 level Multi-AZ deployment - it create one secondary database (backup) under Multi-AZ deployment in 1 different availability zones and if primary database is down it stores in the secondary databases. 

The secondary databases server is usually in the readonly mode when primary server is active and available (Read/Write mode). Once the primary serve isn''t available, the secondary database mode  becomes read/write mode.</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby replica of the database in a different Availability Zone. If the primary or its AZ fails, RDS automatically fails over to the standby with minimal downtime, improving availability and durability (it is not for read scaling - that is what read replicas are for).</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Multi-AZ = high availability/automatic failover; read replicas = scaling reads.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#dc2626">❌ Wrong</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">21</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">A CloudWatch alarm can trigger EC2 Auto Scaling actions, for example adding instances when average CPU is high.</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">True</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Target/step scaling policies are driven by CloudWatch alarms on metrics like CPUUtilization.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">22</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">An interface VPC endpoint connects your VPC privately to a supported service using which technology?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">AWS PrivateLink with an elastic network interface inside your subnet</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">AWS PrivateLink with an elastic network interface inside your subnet</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Interface endpoints are ENIs with private IPs powered by PrivateLink; gateway endpoints (S3, DynamoDB) are the other endpoint type.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">23</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which CloudWatch feature watches a metric and notifies you or triggers an action when it crosses a threshold?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">CloudWatch Alarms</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">CloudWatch Alarms</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> Alarms fire on thresholds (e.g. CPUUtilization > 80%) and can send SNS notifications or trigger Auto Scaling.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">24</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">Which EBS volume type is a general-purpose SSD offering a balance of price and performance (and is commonly the default)?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">gp3 (General Purpose SSD)</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">gp3 (General Purpose SSD)</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> gp3 suits most workloads with baseline 3,000 IOPS and 125 MB/s; io2 targets high-performance databases; st1/sc1 are HDD.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr>
      <tr style="background:#f0fdf4;page-break-inside:avoid;">
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;width:36px;">25</td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#111827;margin-bottom:6px;">In a Site-to-Site VPN, what is the role of the Virtual Private Gateway (VGW)?</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Your Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The role of the Virtual Private Gateway is that it creates a secure tunnel over the Internet, where two or more devices can communicate with each other. But, in our case, we have created VGW to connect the machines from the Azure VNG. While connecting the VGW, we configured the Site to Site VPN where we have provided the Azure Vnet IP address CIDR range. Where we actually create the VPN for the connection to Private Internal IP address of Azure VM to this AWS EC2 Machine And In the end, we add this in the Route Table and select Virtual Private Gateway for the connection. And we were able to ping or communicate between AWS EC2 instance and Azure VM.</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;margin-bottom:4px;"><strong>Correct Answer:</strong> <span style="color:#059669">The Virtual Private Gateway is the VPN concentrator on the AWS side. It is attached to your VPC, terminates the two IPsec tunnels coming from your on-premises customer gateway, and routes the encrypted traffic into the VPC.</span></div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;background:#eff6ff;border-left:3px solid #4f46e5;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;color:#1e3a8a;margin-top:6px;"><strong>💡 Explanation:</strong> The pair is: customer gateway on-premise, VGW on AWS, with two tunnels for redundancy between them.</div>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;vertical-align:top;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;color:#059669">✅ Correct</td>
      </tr></tbody>
